Here's my performance analysis of `Truncation.cs` — this is a hot-path file called on every displayed string for thousands of concurrent users.

---

## Scan Execution Checklist

| Recipe | Hits |
|--------|------|
| Unsealed classes | 3 (`FixedLengthTruncator`, `FixedNumberOfCharactersTruncator`, `FixedNumberOfWordsTruncator`) |
| `sealed class` | 0 |
| `.Split(` | 1 (line 178) |
| LINQ `.Take`/`.Skip` | 2 (lines 187, 190) |
| `.TrimEnd()` chained | 1 (line 72) |
| `new List<` / `List<char>[]` | 1 (line 244) |
| `string.Join` | 2 (lines 187, 190) |
| `.ToLower()`/`.ToUpper()` | 0 |
| `.Replace(` | 0 |
| `params` | 0 |

---

## Findings

#### 1. String.Split allocates array on every call (1 instance)
**Impact:** Allocates a `string[]` + N substring objects per invocation — devastating at thousands of calls/sec  
**Files:** Truncation.cs:L178  
**Fix:** Use `MemoryExtensions.Split` on a `ReadOnlySpan<char>` for zero-allocation word counting (.NET 9+), or use a manual index-based scan to find word boundaries without allocating.

```csharp
// Replace Split + LINQ with a span-based word scan
if (truncateFrom == TruncateFrom.Right)
{
    int wordCount = 0;
    int lastEnd = 0;
    var span = value.AsSpan();
    for (int i = 0; i < span.Length; i++)
    {
        if (span[i] == ' ' || i == span.Length - 1)
        {
            if (i > lastEnd || (i == span.Length - 1 && span[i] != ' '))
            {
                wordCount++;
                if (wordCount == length)
                {
                    int end = (i == span.Length - 1 && span[i] != ' ') ? i + 1 : i;
                    return string.Concat(span[..end], truncationString.AsSpan());
                }
            }
            lastEnd = i + 1;
        }
    }
    return value;
}
```

#### 2. Double/triple allocation in right-truncation path (1 instance)
**Impact:** 3 allocations per call: substring via range, `.TrimEnd()` creates another string, then `+` concatenates  
**Files:** Truncation.cs:L72  
**Fix:** Use `AsSpan` + `TrimEnd()` (which is allocation-free on spans) + `string.Concat`:

```csharp
var slice = value.AsSpan(0, length - truncationString.Length).TrimEnd();
return string.Concat(slice, truncationString.AsSpan());
```

#### 3. LINQ `.Take()`/`.Skip()` + `string.Join` on hot path (2 instances)
**Impact:** Allocates LINQ enumerator objects + intermediate collections on every call  
**Files:** Truncation.cs:L187, L190  
**Fix:** Eliminated by rewriting with span-based word scanning (see finding #1).

#### 4. Unsealed classes (3 of 3 non-static classes)
**Impact:** Prevents JIT devirtualization of `ITruncator.Truncate` calls — virtual dispatch overhead on every invocation  
**Files:** Truncation.cs:L37, L80, L159  
**Fix:** Add `sealed` keyword to `FixedLengthTruncator`, `FixedNumberOfCharactersTruncator`, `FixedNumberOfWordsTruncator`

#### 5. `List<char>[]` for static lookup data (1 instance)
**Impact:** Heap-allocated `List<char>` objects with O(n) linear scan per `Contains` call — `IsSymbol` iterates up to 25 chars across 5 lists  
**Files:** Truncation.cs:L244-L251, L259-L268, L278-L287  
**Fix:** Replace with a `SearchValues<char>` (.NET 8+) or a `HashSet<char>` for O(1) lookup. For group membership, use a `FrozenDictionary<char, int>`:

```csharp
private static readonly SearchValues<char> AllSymbols =
    SearchValues.Create("()[]{}+-*/=<>!.,;:?!@#$%^&~`\"'\\|");

internal static bool IsSymbol(char c) => AllSymbols.Contains(c);
```

